text:
No — not from the title itself. `slugify()` appends a random suffix:

```ts
// src/article/article.service.ts:201
slugify(title: string) {
  return slug(title, {lower: true}) + '-' + (Math.random() * Math.pow(36, 6) | 0).toString(36)
}
```

Two articles titled "How to Center a Div" become `how-to-center-a-div-k3j9f2` and `how-to-center-a-div-8zq1mx`. Collision odds are roughly 1 in 2.2 billion per pair; with the birthday bound you'd need ~50k articles sharing one title before you hit even a 50/50 chance.

**But nothing enforces uniqueness, and there's a direct way to force a collision.**

1. **No unique constraint.** `src/article/article.entity.ts:11-12` is a plain `@Column()`. The DB will happily hold two rows with the same slug, and every lookup is `findOne({slug})` — it silently returns whichever row the query planner hands back first. No error, just the wrong article.

2. **`PUT /articles/:slug` writes the slug verbatim from the request body.**
   ```ts
   // src/article/article.service.ts:190-195
   let toUpdate = await this.articleRepository.findOne({ slug: slug});
   let updated = Object.assign(toUpdate, articleData);
   ```
   `articleData` is `@Body('article')` untouched. `CreateArticleDto` has no validation decorators and the update handler has no `@UsePipes(new ValidationPipe())` — only `POST /users` and `POST /users/login` do. So `{"article": {"slug": "someone-elses-slug-a1b2c3"}}` just lands. There's also no ownership check on that route, so any authenticated user can point any article's slug at any other. (Same `Object.assign` will overwrite `id`, `created`, and `favoriteCount` too.)

So: accidental collision, practically never. Deliberate collision, one request.

Two smaller things in that same line, while you're looking at it:

- **`| 0` overflows.** `36^6` is 2,176,782,336 but `|0` is a 32-bit *signed* cast, so ~1.35% of draws (anything ≥ 2^31) wrap negative and `toString(36)` prefixes a `-`. Those slugs come out as `my-title--zik0zj`. Harmless in a URL, but that's why you'd occasionally see a double hyphen.
- **The suffix isn't zero-padded**, so a small draw gives `my-title-5`. Still unique, just inconsistent-looking.

And a related known gap: `update()` never regenerates the slug on a title change — that's the `// Todo: update slug also when title gets changed` at `src/article/article.controller.ts:61`. So the slug reflects whatever the title was at creation.

Want me to add the unique index and strip `slug`/`id` from the update payload?
